Documentation for mmig_ppg
--------------------------
rev 0.1, 2/2/99
rev 0.3, 2/10/99  Replaced SignExtendInputs with A={Signed,Unsigned,Both} 
                  and B={Signed,Unsigned,Both}
rev 0.6, 3/9/99   Updated with final MMI generic icons
rev 0.7, 4/22/99  Fixed bug in verilog (declaration of btemp)
rev 0.8, 4/30/99  Added netlist_level property to icon, so that it will
                  default to "schematic" (not "icon") in sue's dpc & verilog
                  modes.
rev 0.9, 5/25/99  Added support for ReduceInputLoad parameter
rev 1.0, 5/27/99  underlying generators changed
rev 1.1, 7/16/99  Added NAME_ppg proc, which creates ppg's name
rev 1.2, 7/27/99  Fixed calls to underlying generators
rev 1.3, 2/29/00  Upgraded to use generators_utilities
rev 1.4, 5/18/00  Changed name to mmig_ppg
                  Fixed a few nit drawing-style bugs
                  Fixed bug on failed write to behavioral verilog file
rev 1.5, 5/31/00  Changed drawing style for faster viewing/netlisting
rev 1.6, 6/13/00  Added support for Booth Radix 4 and Radix 8 PPG logic.
                  Removed bitslice option.  Renamed Algorithm option
                  to PPGAlgorithm.  Changed HalfHeight and ReduceInputLoad
                  option values from Y/N to 1/0.
rev 1.7, 6/23/00  Added support for OptimizeMulArray=MSB for PPG=Simple.
rev 1.8, 8/08/00  Upgraded to derive B usage info from global data structures


The ppg generator is used to derive partial product generation logic
for a multiplier.

GENERATOR ARGUMENTS:
--------------------
  NBITS_A:
    Definition:
      The number of bits in input value A_H.  The number of partial 
      products generated will be NBITS_A + NBITS_B.  The width of each
      partial product will be max(NBITS_A, NBITS_B).
    Legal Values:
      1 <= NBITS_A; NBITS_A + NBITS_B <= 128
    Default:
      8

  NBITS_B:
    Definition:
      The number of bits in input value B_H.  The number of partial 
      products generated will be NBITS_A + NBITS_B.  The width of each
      partial product will be max(NBITS_A, NBITS_B).
    Legal Values:
      1 <= NBITS_B; NBITS_A + NBITS_B <= 128 
    Default:
      8

  A:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify whether the multiplier's A
      (multiplier) input is signed or unsigned.  The user can choose Signed,
      Unsigned, or Both.  If Signed is chosen, the generated multiplier will
      always treat the A input as a signed number.  If Unsigned is chose, the
      generated multiplier will always treat the A input as an unsigned number.
      If Both is chosen, logic is included to allow the A input to be treated
      as either signed or unsigned, based on the ASigned_H control signal.
      NOTE:  there are area and speed consequences to these choices.  Unsigned
        is the smallest and fastest.  Signed is very slightly larger and slower.
        Both is noticeably larger and SIGNIFICANTLY slower, and requires the use
        of the ASigned_H control signal which drives across the width of the 
        multiplier.  It is highly recommended that you choose with care.
      NOTE:  If Signed or Both is desired, NOT(A[msb]) needs to be injected 
        to a carry in bit in the multiplier array in order to correctly perform 
        a multiplication of a negative value of A.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Signed", "Unsigned", and "Both".
    Default:
      Unsigned
      
  B:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify whether the multiplier's B
      (multiplicand) input is signed or unsigned.  The user can choose Signed,
      Unsigned, or Both.  If Signed is chosen, the generated multiplier will
      always treat the B input as a signed number.  If Unsigned is chose, the
      generated multiplier will always treat the B input as an unsigned number.
      If Both is chosen, logic is included to allow the B input to be treated
      as either signed or unsigned, based on the BSigned_H control signal.
      NOTE:  there are area and speed consequences to these choices.  Unsigned
        is the smallest and fastest.  Signed is very slightly larger and slower.
        Both is noticeably larger and SIGNIFICANTLY slower, and requires the use
        of the BSigned_H control signal which drives across the width of the 
        multiplier.  It is highly recommended that you choose with care.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are "Signed", "Unsigned", and "Both".
    Default:
      Unsigned

  AddExtraCSA:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify that an extra row of carry 
      save (4:2) adders be included in the multiplier array.  In addition,
      two extra input vectors (CSA_A_H and CSA_B_H), each of width NBITS_A 
      + NBITS_B, are provided as the additional inputs to this final row of 
      adders.
      This feature allows the user to implement a multiply-adder, or multiply-
      accumulator.
      NOTE:  This parameter is needed in this generator only for the purpose
      of accessing global variables.  Its value does not affect the circuits
      produced by this generator.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 0 and 1.
    Default:
      0

  OptimizeMulArray:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify various optimizations
      to the multiplier array.  
      OptimizeMulArray=None:  perform no optimizations
      OptimizeMulArray=Lsb or Both:  remove 0-inputs to bits below the result's
        "NBITS_A" bit position.  This option may speed up the evaluation
        of the less significant result bits.
      OptimizeMulArray=Msb or Both:  modifies how sign extension is
        done in the partial products.  May significantly reduce the 
        loading on some input signals.  Currently implemented only for
        PPGAlgorithm=Simple.
      Note that if IncludePPG = 1 and PPGAlgorithm != Simple, then
      OptimizeMulArray must have the value "Lsb".
    Legal Values:
      None, Lsb, Msb, Both
    Default:
      None

  PPGAlgorithm:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify the partial product
      generation algorithm.
    Legal Values:
      Simple, BoothRadix4, BoothRadix8
    Default:
      Simple

  ReduceInputLoad:
    Definition:
      This argument is passed to the ppg_slice and nandbuf generators,
      where it causes them to reduce the sizes of the gates which 
      connect to the A and B inputs.  This may slow down the circuits.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 0 and 1.
    Default:
      0

  HalfHeight:
    Definition:
      This argument allows the user to specify that the layout be done in
      (NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 bit positions, instead of (NBITS_A + NBITS_B) 
      bit positions.  Note that if (NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 is an odd number of
      bit positions, the layout will actually fit in 
      ((NBITS_A + NBITS_B)/2 + 1) bit positions.
      This configuration results in a slightly slower and less area 
      efficient design.
    Legal Values:
      The allowed values of this argument are 1 (half height layout)
      0 (full height layout).
    Default:
      0



INPUT SIGNALS:
--------------
PPGAlgorithm = Simple:
  A_H (multiplier) and B_H (multiplicand)
  If A = Both:
    ASigned_H
  If B = Both:
    BSigned_H

PPGAlgorithm = BoothRadix4:
  B_H (multiplicand)
  PPSel*_H (partial product selects)

PPGAlgorithm = BoothRadix8:
  B_H (multiplicand)
  B3_H (3 * multiplicand)
  PPSel*_H (partial product selects)


OUTPUT SIGNALS:
---------------
  PP*_H (partial product vectors)


